---
// Privacy for Findish, at /privacy/: the search page, the MCP server
// for AI assistants, the Grocery Deals list lookup and the weekly program. This is the
// privacy policy URL for the Connectors Directory listing, so it covers what's collected,
// how it's used and stored, who it's shared with, how long it's kept and who to contact.
// If anything here changes (a new service, a new kind of data, Session Replay's rate),
// change this page and its date in the same piece of work.
import LabLayout from 'kp-design/layouts/LabLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
import '../styles/theme.css';
import '../styles/prose.css';
import { ASSISTANTS_PAGE } from '../lib/assistants';

const updated = 'November 16, 2026';
---

<LabLayout
  site={SITE}
  bar={false}
  lab={{ name: 'the search', href: '/' }}
  about="/about/"
  title="Findish privacy"
  description="What Findish and its server for AI assistants collect, what stays on your device, who it’s shared with and how long it’s kept."
  image="/og-privacy.png"
  imageAlt="Findish privacy: no accounts, no ads, nothing sold."
  themeColor={{ light: '#f7f8f6', dark: '#151918' }}
>
  <article class="prose" data-page="privacy">
    <h1>Findish privacy</h1>
    <p class="updated">For the search page, its server for AI assistants and the weekly program that collects recipes. Updated {updated}.</p>

    <section aria-labelledby="short">
      <h2 id="short">The short version</h2>
      <p>
        There are no accounts, ads or sign-ups, and nothing is sold or shared for advertising. What you type into the search stays on your
        device. The server for AI assistants answers each question and keeps nothing about it. The only thing collected is anonymous usage
        from the search page, and you can turn that off below.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="page">
      <h2 id="page">The search page</h2>
      <p>
        The whole index of recipes downloads to your browser once, and the search runs there. What you type is never sent to this site or
        anyone else.
      </p>
      <p>
        Recipe photos load from each creator’s own site when they come on screen, without telling that site which page you came from. Like
        any website, those sites see your IP address when they send a photo, and they have their own privacy rules. Opening a recipe takes
        you to the creator’s site.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="analytics">
      <h2 id="analytics">Anonymous usage</h2>
      <p>
        Findish uses <a href="https://amplitude.com">Amplitude</a> to see which pages are viewed
        and what gets clicked, so it can be improved. It records things like “searched with 2 ingredients and got 14 results”, “added tofu from the
        suggestions” or “opened the third result, from The Woks of Life”, never the words you typed. It stores no IP address and sets
        no cookies; a random ID in your browser’s storage tells a return visit from a new one, and basic details like browser type and
        screen size come along.
      </p>
      <p>
        About 1 in 20 visits is also recorded as a Session Replay to see where the page is confusing. In those recordings all text and
        everything you type is hidden, so they show only the layout, scrolling and taps.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once a day, the site counts how often each recipe was opened in the last 90 days, so popular recipes get a small boost in results.
        That count is totals only, with nothing about who opened what.
      </p>
      <p>
        The events stay in Amplitude for as long as the site runs, so trends can be compared over time. How Amplitude handles them is in{' '}
        <a href="https://amplitude.com/privacy">Amplitude’s privacy notice</a>.
      </p>
      <h3>Turning it off</h3>
      <p>
        Nothing is sent if your browser sends Global Privacy Control or Do Not Track, or if you turn it off here. The choice covers Findish
        in this browser.
      </p>
      <p class="toggle">
        <button type="button" data-analytics-toggle>Turn off anonymous usage in this browser</button>
        <span role="status" data-analytics-state></span>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="assistants">
      <h2 id="assistants">AI assistants</h2>
      <p>
        When you connect an assistant to the <a href={ASSISTANTS_PAGE}>Findish server</a> and it searches for you, your assistant
        sends this site the words and ingredients for that search. The server answers with recipe names and links and then forgets the
        request. It doesn’t save or log what was asked, it isn’t counted in analytics, and there’s no account, so it can’t tell one person
        from another.
      </p>
      <p>
        Cloudflare, which runs the site, keeps routine records of each request (the time, the address requested, whether it worked and
        network details like an IP address) for a few days, to keep the site running and safe. Those records don’t include what was searched.
      </p>
      <p>Your assistant (Claude, ChatGPT or another) has its own privacy rules for your conversations, including whether it keeps them.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="deals">
      <h2 id="deals">The Seattle Grocery Deals app</h2>
      <p>
        When you ask the Seattle Grocery Deals app for recipes that use several things on your shopping list, it sends this site only the
        ingredients it matched to that week’s deals, never what you typed. The server answers and keeps nothing about it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="sharing">
      <h2 id="sharing">Who else is involved</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing is sold, and nothing is shared for advertising. Two services help run the site and only do that job: Cloudflare hosts it and{' '}
        <a href="https://amplitude.com">Amplitude</a> holds the anonymous usage. Apart from the recipe photos, everything, fonts included, is
        served from this site.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="collecting">
      <h2 id="collecting">The weekly program</h2>
      <p>
        The program that collects recipes reads public recipe pages and newsletters’ public feeds. It keeps a recipe’s name, link, photo
        link, ingredients, time and cuisine, and nothing about the people who read or comment on those sites. How it works, and how a site
        owner can opt out, is on the <a href="/about/#owners">About page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="contact">
      <h2 id="contact">Questions</h2>
      <p>
        Email <a href="mailto:mail@kylepace.net?subject=Findish%20privacy">mail@kylepace.net</a>. If anything here changes, this page
        and its date will change with it.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

  <script>
    // The opt-out switch. kp-design keeps the choice in this browser's storage, which is per
    // site (findish.now). Under GPC or Do Not Track nothing is sent anyway, and it says so.
    import { isOptedOut, setOptOut } from 'kp-design/analytics';
    const button = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-analytics-toggle]')!;
    const state = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-analytics-state]')!;
    const nav = navigator as Navigator & { globalPrivacyControl?: boolean };
    const browserSaysNo = nav.globalPrivacyControl === true || nav.doNotTrack === '1';
    function show() {
      const off = isOptedOut();
      button.textContent = off ? 'Turn anonymous usage back on in this browser' : 'Turn off anonymous usage in this browser';
      state.textContent = browserSaysNo
        ? 'Your browser asks sites not to track it, so nothing is sent either way.'
        : off
          ? 'Off. Nothing is sent from this browser.'
          : 'On.';
    }
    button.addEventListener('click', () => {
      setOptOut(!isOptedOut());
      show();
    });
    show();
  </script>
</LabLayout>

<style>
  .updated {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .toggle {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
  }

  .toggle button {
    min-height: 2.75rem;
    padding: 0 1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--field-rule);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    background: var(--bg-raised);
    color: var(--ink);
    font: inherit;
    font-weight: 600;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .toggle button:hover {
    border-color: var(--ink);
  }
</style>
